{"id":"f7e3a8e5-2d99-49b1-80ec-a65f959555da","object":"chat.completion","created":1785032558,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    lengths = case['lengths']\\n    scores = case['scores']\\n    budget = case['budget']\\n    n = len(lengths)\\n    # dp: dict mapping tokens_used -> (score, tokens_used, indices_tuple)\\n    dp = {0: (0.0, 0, ())}\\n    for i in range(n):\\n        l = lengths[i]\\n        s = scores[i]\\n        new_dp = dp.copy()  # start with not taking i\\n        for b, (sc, tok, idx) in dp.items():\\n            nb = b + l\\n            if nb > budget:\\n                continue\\n            new_sc = sc + s\\n            new_tok = tok + l\\n            new_idx = idx + (i,)\\n            # Compare with existing in new_dp at nb\\n            if nb in new_dp:\\n                old_sc, old_tok, old_idx = new_dp[nb]\\n                # Better: larger score, then smaller tokens, then lexicographically smaller indices\\n                if new_sc > old_sc + 1e-12:\\n                    better = True\\n                elif abs(new_sc - old_sc) <= 1e-12:\\n                    if new_tok < old_tok:\\n                        better = True\\n                    elif new_tok == old_tok:\\n                        if new_idx < old_idx:\\n                            better = True\\n                        else:\\n                            better = False\\n                    else:\\n                        better = False\\n                else:\\n                    better = False\\n            else:\\n                better = True\\n            if better:\\n                new_dp[nb] = (new_sc, new_tok, new_idx)\\n        dp = new_dp\\n    # Find best overall among all budgets up to budget\\n    best_score = -float('inf')\\n    best_tok = 0\\n    best_idx = ()\\n    for b, (sc, tok, idx) in dp.items():\\n        if sc > best_score + 1e-12:\\n            best_score = sc\\n            best_tok = tok\\n            best_idx = idx\\n        elif abs(sc - best_score) <= 1e-12:\\n            if tok < best_tok:\\n                best_tok = tok\\n                best_idx = idx\\n            elif tok == best_tok and idx < best_idx:\\n                best_idx = idx\\n    selected = list(best_idx)\\n    return {\\n        'selected_indices': selected,\\n        'tokens_used': best_tok,\\n        'total_score': best_score\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to parse the task. The user provides a fixture payload with cases. The function solve(case) is to be implemented in Python. The case is the inner payload object (e.g., {lengths, scores, budget, _case_nonce}). We need to return {selected_indices: int list, tokens_used: int, total_score: number}. The problem: bounded budget selection of sentences with token lengths and scores, preserve order, maximize sum score, break ties by fewer tokens used, then lexicographic indices. Use DP (knapsack with ordering constraint: we must pick a subsequence, not arbitrary subset? Probably yes, because sentences are in document order and we must preserve order, but we could skip any sentences. That's essentially a knapsack with items in order, but we can choose any subset, order preserved by indices. That's just standard 0-1 knapsack with order constraint: we can choose any subset, but the selected indices will naturally be in increasing order. The DP can be over items and budget. Since we need to maximize sum scores, and break ties by smaller tokens_used (i.e., if sum scores equal, prefer solution with less total tokens), then if still tie, lexicographic compare of selected indices (presumably ascending order? Lexicographic meaning compare first element, then second, etc. Usually smaller indices preferred. Since we are selecting a subset, the list of indices sorted ascending. Lexicographically smaller means that at the first differing position, the index is smaller. So we need to implement DP